WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:02.600
- [Narrator] Land-based
ballistic missiles,

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.440
submarine-launched weaponry

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.200
and bombs that can be
deployed from aircraft.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.370
These are some of the weapons

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.450
in Russia's vast nuclear arsenal,

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.840
seen here on display in training drills

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.363
before Russia launched
its invasion of Ukraine.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:17.640
- The strategic systems,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.690
they have intercontinental range.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.780
And so they can hit anywhere on the globe.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.430
- [Narrator] While the US
doesn't communicate much

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:25.900
on its nuclear weapons,

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.222
Russia has been showcasing its advances.

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:30.095
(waves crashing)

00:00:30.095 --> 00:00:34.762
(reporter speaking in foreign language)

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:37.790
- [Narrator] After President Putin ordered

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.640
Moscow's nuclear deterrence forces

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.940
to be put on alert in later February,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.950
the Pentagon said it had not detected

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.630
any major new developments on that front.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.090
Many analysts say it's
unlikely the weapons

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.940
will be used in warfare,
although it can't be ruled out.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.400
But Moscow could use its
arsenal as a deterrent

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.360
to discourage the US and its allies

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.930
from taking more action
in the Ukraine war.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.000
So here's what we know about
Russia's nuclear inventory,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.550
the largest in the world

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.473
and Putin's push to modernize it.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:07.830
When it comes to scale,

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.360
Russia and the US have a similar
number of nuclear weapons.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.220
According to an estimate by the Federation

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.910
of American Scientists,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:19.163
Russia's warhead inventory totals 5,977.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:24.000
In comparison, the US has 5,428.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.320
Those numbers are similar also due

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.770
to international arms control agreements

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.110
to limit the size of each
country's nuclear stockpile.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.680
At the heart of Moscow's arsenal

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.750
is what military experts
call the nuclear triad.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.180
The US also has nuclear triad status,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.330
while China has been working

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.780
to achieve the same advanced
version in recent years.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.620
In principle, Russia's weapons
can be delivered by air

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.000
with strategic bombers,
such as the TU-95MS

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.160
and TU-160 by its navy

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.830
with nuclear-powered
ballistic missile submarines

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.030
and by land-based ballistic launchers,

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.093
such as this RS-24 YARS.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.960
The explosive force of atomic bombs

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.740
is measured in kilotons

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.150
and warheads on these weapons have a power

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.700
of as much as 400 kilotons according

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.550
to defense analysts.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.370
That's more than 25 times
the atomic bomb the US used

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.779
in Hiroshima, Japan at
the end of World War II.

00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:20.180
(pensive music)

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.220
Days after the first bomb was released,

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.320
another was dropped on Nagasaki,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.410
seen here in this footage in 1945.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.350
It became the last atomic
bomb to be used in combat.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.440
Since then, countries
have built up arsenals

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.300
as a deterrent for conflict.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.700
The US and Russia created
their vast stockpiles

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.993
during the Cold War.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.960
- And our restraint is not inexhaustible.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.060
- [Narrator] More recently,
and decades after the end

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.820
of that Cold War, Moscow is again touting

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.190
that its intercontinental
ballistic missiles

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:52.550
are capable of hitting the
US in 20 to 30 minutes.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.210
Hans Kristensen is the director

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.140
of the Nuclear Information Project

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.740
and has been studying
nuclear weapons for decades.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.290
He says long-range missiles

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.870
are the most powerful
elements in Russia's arsenal.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.560
- And if that was released,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.603
it could destroy all major cities.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.540
- [Narrator] The US also
has missiles capable

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.920
of potentially hitting Russia
within a similar timeframe.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.300
In addition, both
countries have stockpiles

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.630
of non-strategic nuclear weapons,

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.390
which are usually defined as
shorter-range delivery systems

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.520
with lower-yield warheads.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.030
But analysts note as the
US has been scaling back

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:28.220
its stockpile of these weapons
over the last three decades,

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.000
Russia has largely kept its inventory.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.600
- We think the US has
only a small inventory

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.260
of a few hundred tactical
nuclear bombs that are left.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.960
This has been an important development

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.610
in the US arsenal evolution

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.560
because they used to have, like Russia,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.210
a huge inventory of non-strategic

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.010
but they've largely moved away from that

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.480
in the US planning.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.450
- [Narrator] Russia is modernizing
some Soviet era weapons,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.530
which analysts say is a routine step

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.540
in order to maintain the
readiness of older tools.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.500
It's also developing newer weapons

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.490
that have been publicized

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.570
by Russian President Vladimir Putin

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.693
and on Russian state TV.

00:04:03.693 --> 00:04:08.693
(report speaking in foreign language)

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.950
- [Narrator] Zircon is
one of six nuclear weapons

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.670
Putin revealed the country
was developing in March 2018.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.570
Others included underwater
nuclear-powered uncrewed vehicles

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.850
and nuclear-powered global
range cruise missiles.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.530
Analysts say it's hard to determine

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.577
what stage of development
they are currently at.

00:04:26.577 --> 00:04:28.940
(whooshing sound)

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:29.980
Unlike the US,

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.450
Russia has been sharing
its nuclear capabilities

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.000
on state TV and online media.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Wary of stoking tensions
as war rages in Ukraine,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.470
US officials even canceled
a scheduled weapon test.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.080
Although other exercises continued.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.550
In mid-March, the US Air
Force conducted drilled

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.370
with B-52 bombers,

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.573
capable of carrying nuclear missiles.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.400
But to use nuclear weapons
would be a significant step.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.600
Some experts say Putin may
turn to non-strategic bombs

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.400
if cornered in this war.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.380
However, the bar is high
for any potential deployment

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.150
and it's unlikely that
Russia will use them at all.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.010
- It's one thing to rattle the sword

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.930
and warn your adversaries
that you have this capability

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.610
by doing exercises or increasing alert

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:15.760
or something like that.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.130
It's another step to
actually use nuclear weapons

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.380
because once you do,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.380
all bets are off in terms of
how destructive this new phase

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.330
is going to be.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.450
- [Narrator] The buildup and
showcasing of new weapons

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:35.050
during the Ukraine war reminds
some of the Cold War era,

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.660
a period when the US and
Russia used ownership

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.517
of nuclear forces as a defense
amid heightened tensions.

00:05:41.517 --> 00:05:44.184
(pensive music)

